<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tortuga</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1665 CE (approx.) · capital Basse-Terre (Tortuga)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rocky island off northern Hispaniola that became the most famous base of the Caribbean buccaneers before France turned it into the seed of Saint-Domingue.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From around 1630 French and English hunters, the boucaniers who smoked wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> meat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, used Tortuga as a refuge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> raided it several times, in 1635 and 1638 among others. In 1640 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-le-vasseur">Jean Le Vasseur</a> took control on behalf of the French governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-de-la-roche">Fort de la Roche</a> above Basse-Terre harbour.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Government was loose and personal: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-le-vasseur">Le Vasseur</a> ruled almost as an independent lord until his murder in 1653. The island's economy rested on trading hides and meat, and increasingly on buccaneering raids against Spanish shipping and towns, with the plunder sold in its harbour. Settlers were a mix of French Catholics and Protestants, English adventurers and indentured servants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a final Spanish occupation in 1654 to 1655, French control returned. In 1665 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-dogeron">Bertrand d'Ogeron</a> as governor, who encouraged settlers to plant on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> mainland. Tortuga faded as the capital moved across the water to the new colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1630 CE – 1665 CE (approx.)">